      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages instead of every stage, so it uses fewer flip-flops and less area than pipelined for the same n_iter and data_width, while still delivering 1 result/cycle (meeting the 32 Msps throughput constraint). The latency is ceil(N/m)+2 cycles; with N=12-14 and m=2-4, latency is 5-9 cycles, well within the 0.5 us batch window at any plausible Fmax. The sys_p99_batch_us constraint requires all 32 burst results back within 0.5 us of the tick; at 1 result/cycle the burst takes 32 cycles, so Fmax must be >= 64 MHz, which is easily met. I search m=2-4 to find the area sweet spot: m=2 halves register count vs pipelined, m=4 quarters it but adds combinational depth that may lower Fmax. Same accuracy-critical ranges as pipelined since the bit-accurate computation is identical."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The fleet facts show that only pipelined has found feasible designs (71 of 100 evals, best area 39973 um^2), and it owns all 17 designs on the merged front. iterative and unrolled_k found zero feasible designs, confirming that the 32 Msps throughput constraint (>=32 results/us) rules out multi-cycle-per-result architectures. My two families, pipelined and pipelined_m, are the only ones that produce 1 result/cycle. The accuracy constraint max_abs_err <= 2^-12 = 0.000244141 is the binding accuracy requirement; from the first round, feasible designs cluster around data_width 14-16 and n_iter 12-14. The area objective pushes toward the minimum feasible data_width and n_iter, so I narrow the ranges to this corner to let Optuna resolve the exact boundary. For pipelined_m, the m parameter (2-4) offers a direct area reduction over pipelined by reducing register count, while maintaining 1-result/cycle throughput. The sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 us constraint requires the 32-request burst to complete within 0.5 us; at 1 result/cycle this needs Fmax >= 64 MHz, which is comfortably achievable. I allocate 60% of budget to pipelined (the proven front owner) and 40% to pipelined_m (the area-reduction variant that may push the front further in the area direction). The tight ranges focus the 100-eval budget on the trade-off region rather than wasting evaluations on obviously infeasible or dominated corners."
}

